ts, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten-thousands) the same number of times. Since there are 120 numbers and 5 positions, each digit will appear:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{120}{5} = 24 \\text{ times in each position.}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the contribution of each digit to the total sum in each place value:**\n   - For the ten-thousands place: Each digit contributes \\(10000 \\times 24\\).\n   - For the thousands place: Each digit contributes \\(1000 \\times 24\\).\n   - For the hundreds place: Each digit contributes \\(100 \\times 24\\).\n   - For the tens place: Each digit contributes \\(10 \\times 24\\).\n   - For the units place: Each digit contributes \\(1 \\times 24\\).\n\n4. **Sum the contributions for all digits in all positions:**\n   The sum of the digits \\(1, 3, 5, 7, 8\\) is:\n   \\[\n   1 + 3 + 5 + 7 + 8 = 24\n   \\]\n   Therefore, the total sum of all 5-digit numbers is:\n   \\[\n   24 \\times (10000 + 1000 + 100 + 10 + 1) \\times 24 = 24 \\times 11111 \\times 24\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the total sum:**\n   \\[\n   24 \\times 11111 \\times 24 = 24^2 \\times 11111 = 576 \\times 11111 = 6399936\n   \\]\n\n6. **Find the average of all 5-digit numbers:**\n   The average is the total sum divided by the number of 5-digit numbers:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Average} = \\frac{6399936}{120} = 53332.8\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the average (mean) of all 5-digit numbers that can be formed using the digits \\(1\\), \\(3\\), \\(5\\), \\(7\\), and \\(8\\) exactly once is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{53332.8}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{53332.8}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true}}
